# SelectoUpdato: Atomic Nested Writes

## Overview

SelectoUpdato 0.4 restores nested writes with a different ownership boundary:
the domain describes an owned relationship, Updato compiles a portable atomic
`Selecto.Write.Graph`, and the configured database adapter owns generated-key
propagation, SQL strategy, transaction behavior, and rollback.

This workbook runs a nested insert and owned-child synchronization against
isolated PostgreSQL temporary tables. It uses Postgrex directly—there is no
`Ecto.Repo`, schema, changeset, or Updato-owned SQL path.

```elixir
Code.require_file(Path.expand("support/bootstrap.exs", __DIR__))
SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.install_updato!()
```

## 1) The new finite-model coverage

The portable-command model crosses 48 states with nine invariants. The
nested-graph model crosses 24 states with six invariants. Together with the
write-scope and action-capability models, current Updato executes 688 bounded
checks.

```elixir
portable_report = SelectoUpdato.Verification.PortableCommandSafety.verify()
nested_report = SelectoUpdato.Verification.NestedGraphSafety.verify()

%{state_count: 48, invariant_count: 9, check_count: 432, proved?: true} =
  Map.take(portable_report, [:state_count, :invariant_count, :check_count, :proved?])

%{state_count: 24, invariant_count: 6, check_count: 144, proved?: true} =
  Map.take(nested_report, [:state_count, :invariant_count, :check_count, :proved?])
```

## 2) One Postgrex session and two isolated tables

Temporary tables belong to one PostgreSQL session, so the direct connection
uses a one-process pool.

```elixir
connection_config =
  SelectoLivebooksNotebookBootstrap.repo_config()
  |> Keyword.put(:pool_size, 1)

{:ok, connection} = Postgrex.start_link(connection_config)

run_suffix = System.unique_integer([:positive, :monotonic])
orders_table = "updato_nested_orders_#{run_suffix}"
items_table = "updato_nested_items_#{run_suffix}"

Postgrex.query!(connection, """
CREATE TEMP TABLE #{orders_table} (
  id bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  tenant_id bigint NOT NULL,
  reference text NOT NULL
)
""", [])

Postgrex.query!(connection, """
CREATE TEMP TABLE #{items_table} (
  id bigserial PRIMARY KEY,
  tenant_id bigint NOT NULL,
  order_id bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES #{orders_table}(id),
  sku text NOT NULL,
  quantity integer NOT NULL
)
""", [])

tenant_id = 91_001
```

## 3) Complete parent and child contracts

Field policy is colocated with each source column, and relationship policy is
colocated with the source association it governs. Selecto normalizes those
authoring conveniences into canonical `writes.fields` and
`writes.relationships` before Updato compiles the graph. Each level still has
its own required insert fields, tenant scope, and cardinality contract. The
caller may submit `items`, but may not submit `order_id`: the graph binds that
ownership key from the generated parent `id`.

```elixir
item_domain = %{
  name: "Nested order items",
  source: %{
    source_table: items_table,
    primary_key: :id,
    fields: [:id, :tenant_id, :order_id, :sku, :quantity],
    columns: %{
      id: %{type: :integer, write: %{server_managed: true}},
      tenant_id: %{type: :integer, write: %{insertable: true, immutable: true}},
      order_id: %{type: :integer},
      sku: %{
        type: :string,
        write: %{insertable: true, updatable: true, required_on: [:insert]}
      },
      quantity: %{
        type: :integer,
        write: %{insertable: true, updatable: true, required_on: [:insert]}
      }
    },
    associations: %{}
  },
  schemas: %{},
  joins: %{},
  writes: %{
    operations: %{
      insert: %{enabled: true, expected_cardinality: {:exactly, 1}},
      update: %{
        enabled: true,
        require_filter: true,
        expected_cardinality: {:exactly, 1}
      }
    },
    scope: %{tenant: %{required: true, field: :tenant_id}},
    relationships: %{}
  }
}

order_domain = %{
  name: "Nested orders",
  source: %{
    source_table: orders_table,
    primary_key: :id,
    fields: [:id, :tenant_id, :reference],
    columns: %{
      id: %{type: :integer, write: %{server_managed: true}},
      tenant_id: %{type: :integer, write: %{insertable: true, immutable: true}},
      reference: %{
        type: :string,
        write: %{insertable: true, updatable: true, required_on: [:insert]}
      }
    },
    associations: %{
      items: %{
        queryable: :items,
        field: :items,
        cardinality: :many,
        owner_key: :id,
        related_key: :order_id,
        write: %{
          writable: true,
          ownership: :owned,
          allowed_ops: [:insert, :update, :delete],
          domain: item_domain,
          identity_fields: [:id],
          strategy: :sync,
          delete_missing: true,
          min_items: 1,
          max_items: 10
        }
      }
    }
  },
  schemas: %{},
  joins: %{},
  writes: %{
    operations: %{
      insert: %{
        enabled: true,
        expected_cardinality: {:exactly, 1},
        returning: :all
      },
      update: %{
        enabled: true,
        require_filter: true,
        expected_cardinality: {:exactly, 1},
        returning: :all
      }
    },
    scope: %{tenant: %{required: true, field: :tenant_id}}
  }
}

selecto = %Selecto{
  domain: order_domain,
  adapter: SelectoDBPostgreSQL.Adapter,
  connection: connection
}
```

## 4) Fail closed before adapter dispatch

Required root fields and generated ownership fields are checked while
compiling portable intent. Neither failure can reach adapter preview or SQL.

```elixir
missing_reference =
  order_domain
  |> SelectoUpdato.new(tenant: %{tenant_id: tenant_id})
  |> SelectoUpdato.insert(%{items: [%{sku: "A", quantity: 2}]})

{:error, %SelectoUpdato.Error{type: :validation}} =
  SelectoUpdato.PortableCommand.compile(missing_reference)

ownership_override =
  order_domain
  |> SelectoUpdato.new(tenant: %{tenant_id: tenant_id})
  |> SelectoUpdato.insert(%{
    reference: "SO-FORGED",
    items: [%{order_id: 999, sku: "A", quantity: 2}]
  })

{:error,
 %SelectoUpdato.Error{
   type: :invalid_graph,
   details: %{field: :order_id}
 }} = SelectoUpdato.PortableCommand.compile(ownership_override)
```

## 5) Compile and preview a generated-key graph

The root row comes first. The child node binds `order_id` from the root's
returned `id`; tenant scope is compiled independently into every row. Preview
is adapter-owned and reports an atomic graph without mutating the database.

```elixir
insert_operation =
  order_domain
  |> SelectoUpdato.new(tenant: %{tenant_id: tenant_id})
  |> SelectoUpdato.insert(%{
    reference: "SO-100",
    items: [
      %{sku: "A", quantity: 2},
      %{sku: "B", quantity: 1}
    ]
  })
  |> SelectoUpdato.returning([:id, :reference])

{:ok, %Selecto.Write.Graph{} = insert_graph, %{tenant_id: ^tenant_id}} =
  SelectoUpdato.PortableCommand.compile(insert_operation)

:ok = Selecto.Write.Graph.validate(insert_graph)
["root", "items"] = Enum.map(insert_graph.nodes, & &1.id)

items_node = Enum.find(insert_graph.nodes, &(&1.id == "items"))

true =
  Enum.all?(items_node.rows, fn row ->
    match?(
      [%Selecto.Write.Graph.Binding{
        field: :order_id,
        from_node: "root",
        from_field: :id
      }],
      row.bindings
    )
  end)

{:ok, insert_preview} = SelectoUpdato.preview(insert_operation, selecto)
true = insert_preview.metadata.atomic?
true = insert_preview.metadata.graph?
insert_preview
```

## 6) Execute the atomic nested insert

The PostgreSQL adapter owns the transaction and generated-key propagation. The
public result contains the root projection; the direct query below verifies
that both children received the generated parent id and trusted tenant.

```elixir
{:ok, inserted} = SelectoUpdato.execute(insert_operation, selecto)
[%{"id" => order_id, "reference" => "SO-100"}] = inserted.rows

%Postgrex.Result{
  rows: [
    [first_item_id, ^tenant_id, ^order_id, "A", 2],
    [second_item_id, ^tenant_id, ^order_id, "B", 1]
  ]
} =
  Postgrex.query!(
    connection,
    "SELECT id, tenant_id, order_id, sku, quantity FROM #{items_table} " <>
      "WHERE order_id = $1 ORDER BY sku",
    [order_id]
  )
```

## 7) Synchronize the complete owned child set

An item with its declared identity compiles as an update; one without identity
compiles as an insert. With `delete_missing: true`, omitting item B removes it.
PostgreSQL 17+ may use a single safe `MERGE`; older servers use the ordered
fallback. The adapter chooses, and the graph remains atomic either way.

```elixir
sync_operation =
  order_domain
  |> SelectoUpdato.new(tenant: %{tenant_id: tenant_id})
  |> SelectoUpdato.filter({:id, order_id})
  |> SelectoUpdato.update(%{
    reference: "SO-100-R",
    items: [
      %{id: first_item_id, sku: "A", quantity: 3},
      %{sku: "C", quantity: 1}
    ]
  })
  |> SelectoUpdato.returning([:id, :reference])

{:ok, %Selecto.Write.Graph{} = sync_graph, _context} =
  SelectoUpdato.PortableCommand.compile(sync_operation)

sync_node = Enum.find(sync_graph.nodes, &(&1.id == "items"))
:sync = sync_node.strategy
[:id] = sync_node.identity_fields
true = sync_node.delete_missing?

{:ok, synced} = SelectoUpdato.execute(sync_operation, selecto)
true = synced.metadata.node_strategies["items"] in [:merge, :ordered_fallback]

%Postgrex.Result{
  rows: [
    [^first_item_id, "A", 3],
    [new_item_id, "C", 1]
  ]
} =
  Postgrex.query!(
    connection,
    "SELECT id, sku, quantity FROM #{items_table} WHERE order_id = $1 ORDER BY sku",
    [order_id]
  )

true = new_item_id not in [first_item_id, second_item_id]
synced
```

## 8) Relationship typos fail before dispatch

The strict relationship validator checks `parent_key`, `child_key`, and
`identity_fields` against their respective domains. A misspelled child key is
an invalid contract, not a database error.

```elixir
invalid_domain =
  put_in(order_domain, [:source, :associations, :items, :write, :child_key], :missing_order_id)

invalid_operation =
  invalid_domain
  |> SelectoUpdato.new(tenant: %{tenant_id: tenant_id})
  |> SelectoUpdato.insert(%{
    reference: "SO-BAD-CONTRACT",
    items: [%{sku: "A", quantity: 1}]
  })

{:error,
 %SelectoUpdato.Error{
   type: :invalid_operation,
   details: %{errors: relationship_errors}
 }} = SelectoUpdato.PortableCommand.compile(invalid_operation)

true =
  Enum.any?(relationship_errors, fn {path, message} ->
    path == [:writes, :relationships, :items, :child_key] and
      message =~ "child domain"
  end)
```

## 9) Cleanup and limits

The current graph contract supports atomic nested insert and update roots with
owned relationships. It deliberately excludes shared-reference mutation,
caller-authored SQL, cross-database graphs, arbitrary callbacks, and
best-effort partial persistence. Nested root updates and existing child rows
currently need at least one writable scalar change.

```elixir
GenServer.stop(connection)
:ok
```
